From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-controller 9993: give node liveness a margin worth the name

A node is aged out after NODE_EXPIRE_TIME seconds without a frame. That was
65 s, while IEEE 1905.1 fixes Topology Discovery at 60 s:

	ieee1905-8.9.13/src/i1905.c, topology_timer_cb()
	    i1905_run_topology_discovery(p);
	    i1905_run_bridge_discovery(p);
	    timer_set(t, 60000);

A ratio of 65/60 = 1.08 means a single lost multicast Discovery removes a node
that is alive and well. Multicast on a Wi-Fi backhaul is exactly the frame that
gets lost.

The removal is not a cosmetic blip. The node re-onboards, floods AP-Autoconfig
Search, and the config churn that follows was measured on hardware as num_nodes
flapping (2026-07-19: "num_nodes oscillating 0<->5, 40x Autoconfig Search in 22 s").
The 5 s of slack was written down as a known hazard back then and never fixed:
"Discovery TX at 60 s vs NODE_EXPIRE_TIME 65 => 5 s of slack = a single lost
multicast frame takes a node down for up to 60 s. Healthy ratio = period ~ 1/3
of the expiry."

180 s is that healthy ratio: three missed Discoveries. For reference, other open
1905 stacks run a 2x ratio - Discovery every 30 s against a 60 s expiry
with a 5 s sweeper. Our sweeper is already finer, 1 s
(cntlr_ageout_nodes from the heartbeat), so only the bound was wrong.

Two properties make this cheap:

  - last_tsp_seen is refreshed by ANY CMDU from a known node, not just by
    Discovery (cntlr_map.c, in cntlr_handle_map_cmdu). ieee1905-rs does the
    same thing deliberately (topology_manager.rs, at the end of
    Ieee1905NodeInfo::update()). A node carrying real traffic is therefore
    refreshed far more often than every 60 s; this bound only governs a node
    that has gone completely silent.
  - The constant has exactly one consumer, cntlr_clean_node().

The cost is deliberate: a node that is genuinely gone now lingers up to 180 s
instead of 65 s. That trade is right because the two errors are not
symmetrical. A false removal costs an onboarding cycle and a config storm on a
healthy network; a late removal costs one stale topology row, and anything that
would act on it - steering above all - must already judge a client by its own
freshness rather than by the presence of its node.

Not fixed here: nothing else. This changes one bound and two comments that
quoted the old value.

--- a/src/cntlr.h
+++ b/src/cntlr.h
@@ -42,7 +42,18 @@
 #define LIMIT_STA_COUNT (100)
 #endif
 
-#define NODE_EXPIRE_TIME 65
+/* A node is declared gone after this many seconds without a single frame
+ * from it. IEEE 1905.1 fixes Topology Discovery at 60 s (ieee1905 i1905.c,
+ * topology_timer_cb: timer_set(t, 60000)), so 65 s left a 5 s margin - one
+ * lost multicast Discovery and a live node was removed. Removal is not
+ * cheap: the node re-onboards, floods Autoconfig Search and churns config.
+ * Three missed Discoveries is the margin; ieee1905-rs uses 2x (30 s TX vs
+ * 60 s expiry). Note last_tsp_seen is refreshed by ANY CMDU from a known
+ * node (cntlr_map.c, cntlr_handle_map_cmdu), so this bound only governs a
+ * node that has gone completely silent. The cost is that a node which is
+ * genuinely gone lingers longer; that is deliberate - steering must judge
+ * a client by its own freshness, never by the mere presence of its node. */
+#define NODE_EXPIRE_TIME 180
 #define METRIC_REP_INTERVAL 10
 
 extern const char *ubus_socket;
--- a/src/cntlr.c
+++ b/src/cntlr.c
@@ -2477,7 +2477,7 @@
 	/* Phase 2 recovery: hydrate the live model from mapc.db so the topology
 	 * is warm at t=0; live discovery confirms-or-overwrites it, and any
 	 * ghost (node powered off while we were down) ages out via the
-	 * existing NODE_EXPIRE_TIME(65s) path. Best-effort; no-op if !db. */
+	 * existing NODE_EXPIRE_TIME path. Best-effort; no-op if !db. */
 	db_restore(c);
 
 	c->state = CNTLR_INIT;
--- a/src/db/mapc_sync.c
+++ b/src/db/mapc_sync.c
@@ -1156,7 +1156,7 @@
 /* On controller boot, hydrate the live model from mapc.db so the topology */
 /* is "warm" at t=0; live discovery only confirms-or-overwrites it, and    */
 /* any ghost (node powered off while the controller was down) ages out via */
-/* the existing NODE_EXPIRE_TIME(65s) path. Inverse of the Phase-1 write   */
+/* the existing NODE_EXPIRE_TIME path. Inverse of the Phase-1 write        */
 /* helpers, using the same engine-neutral mapdb read primitives. Best-     */
 /* effort throughout: c->db == NULL or any prepare/step error -> the graph  */
 /* is simply not seeded and the controller falls back to cold discovery.   */
